<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Relax and Listen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Respond to basic questions about a familiar story. Interactive: Engage in a role-play based on a listened scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can respond to basic questions about nature (e.g., "What can you hear outside?" "I can hear birds." "I can't hear the wind."). Can engage in a role play based on a story related to nature (e.g., "Now we will role play relaxing in nature."). Can respond to questions about a familiar story related to nature (e.g., "Where does the iguana go?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 35%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 45%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 20%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to close their eyes for 10 seconds and listen to the sounds around them, in the classroom or from outside. After 10 seconds, students open their eyes. The teacher shows flashcards of nature elements: <i>bird, tree, flower, grass, sun</i>. The teacher asks, "What can you hear?" and guides responses like "I can hear the birds." or "I can't hear the wind." to activate prior knowledge related to the theme "I Can Relax and Listen." and scenario "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language and pronunciation by saying aloud, "Listen! I can hear a bird. /bɜːrd/." and pointing to the bird flashcard. The teacher then models "I can relax." and takes a deep breath. For phonemic awareness, the teacher clearly pronounces /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun", demonstrating the sounds slowly. The teacher also models blending for "sun" (/s/ /ʌ/ /n/) and "flower" (/f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/). The teacher uses TPR by pretending to listen and pointing to their ear to clarify "listen" and "hear".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use our eyes or ears to listen?" (Ears!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we hear a flower?" (No, we can smell a flower!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short, simple audio story (narrative excerpt, 1-2 minutes long, slow pace) about a child, Leo, visiting a *park* and describing what he can *hear* and *see*. The story incorporates target vocabulary like *bird, tree, grass, flower, sun, sky, river, butterfly, explore, relax, listen, hear, quiet, peaceful, beautiful*, and grammatical structures like "I can hear..." and "I'm outside, near a big tree."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">*Audio Story Example:* "Hello! My name is Leo. Today, I am at the *park*. I'm *outside*, near a big *tree*. The *sun* is bright in the *sky*. I can *listen* to the sounds. Oh! I can *hear* birds singing! Tweet, tweet! I can also hear the *river* flowing. It's so *quiet* and *peaceful* here. I like to *relax* in *nature*. I can see a *beautiful flower* and a *butterfly*. I enjoy being here."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Interpret and respond to simple oral messages related to familiar situations and basic personal needs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio story twice.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (after first listen):</b> "Where is Leo?" (At the park.) "What does Leo like to do there?" (Relax/Listen.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (after second listen):</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students raise a green card for "True" and a red card for "False".</li>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo is inside." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can hear birds." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The park is noisy." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can see a beautiful flower." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific vocabulary (<i>bird, tree, grass, flower, sun, sky, river, park, listen, hear, relax, quiet, peaceful</i>) and grammar (Can/Can't, Prepositions of place: <i>I'm outside</i>, Present simple: <i>I'm happy</i>).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listen and Draw/Match:</b> The teacher says a nature word (e.g., "bird," "tree," "flower," "sun") and students point to the corresponding flashcard or image on their worksheet. Then, the teacher says simple sentences from the story, and students circle the correct picture or draw what they hear. E.g., Teacher says, "I can hear a bird." Students circle the bird. "Leo is near a big tree." Students circle the tree.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays parts of the audio story or reads sentences with a pause for missing words. Students listen and fill in the blanks on a simple worksheet with *can* or *can't*, or target vocabulary words. E.g., "I ___ hear birds singing." (can). "I ___ hear a spider." (can't). "I am in the ___." (park). "It's very ___." (peaceful).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Listen and Guess:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays different nature sounds (e.g., bird chirping, river flowing, wind blowing, leaves rustling). One student listens and says, "I can hear (a bird)." The partner listens and confirms or guesses. The teacher models the polite request: "Can you hear the river, please?" or "What can you hear?" encouraging sensory observations.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play based on a listened scenario.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students will engage in a simple role-play titled "Relaxing in Nature." The teacher sets up a "nature spot" in the classroom using flashcards of a *tree, grass, flowers*, and playing soft nature sounds (birds, river). Students work in pairs or small groups. One student acts as the "person relaxing in nature" (e.g., Leo from the story), closing eyes and listening. The other student acts as a friend who approaches and asks about what they hear. Students take turns asking and responding about sounds and feelings, connecting directly to "I Can Relax and Listen." and "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (listener): "I'm outside. I can hear birds. It's peaceful."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (friend): "What can you hear?" or "Can you hear the river?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "No, I can't hear the river. I can hear the wind."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I like sitting under the tree. It's beautiful here." (Sociolinguistic: Expressing appreciation)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The focus is on fluency in using "Can/Can't" and nature vocabulary through interactive listening and speaking.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the role-play in Stage 4, the teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' listening comprehension and oral production. Students respond to basic questions about the listened scenario and engage in the role-play.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "What can you hear?" with appropriate nature vocabulary.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "I can hear..." and "I can't hear..." correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces target vocabulary and /k/, /s/ intelligibly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in basic role-play interaction (e.g., asks questions, expresses feelings).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows ability to listen and respond in a familiar context related to nature.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by circling "Yes" or "No" and drawing a happy or sad face.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I hear birds and trees?" (Yes / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say what I hear in nature?" (Yes / No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new nature sound did you learn to say today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you enjoy about listening to nature today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to identify and respond to basic nature sounds using "I can hear..." and the target vocabulary. The role-play activity encouraged interaction and the use of the target grammar. Some students needed more prompting for polite requests (e.g., "Can you pass me the binoculars, please?" – although binoculars were not used, the structure was discussed). Adjustments for next lesson: provide more visual support during listening tasks, possibly a simple picture story to accompany the audio to reinforce understanding for visual learners. Ensure more explicit practice of "Can you hear...?" questions in pairs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or dialogue that uses the same nature vocabulary (<i>park, tree, grass, flower, bird, sun, river</i>) and grammatical structures (Can/Can't, Prepositions of place) introduced in this listening lesson, to develop their reading comprehension in context.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>